\title{Stationary d-vine copula processes}
\description{
Stationary d-vine copula process of finite order \eqn{K}, built from a
sequence of pair copulas \eqn{C_1, \ldots, C_K} (independence beyond lag
\eqn{K}).
}
\details{
There are two modes.

\strong{Tied mode} (the only mode that can be fitted). A known Gaussian
ARMA/SARMA \link[=armacop-class]{armacop} model (or an \link[=arfactcop-class]{arfactcop} model) supplies the Kendall partial
autocorrelation function (kpacf) \eqn{\tau_k = (2/\pi)\arcsin\alpha_k},
with \eqn{\alpha_k} the partial autocorrelation of the ARMA process at lag
\eqn{k}. The pair copula at lag \eqn{k} is a member of a family chosen for
that lag, calibrated to have Kendall's tau \eqn{\tau_k}. Lags
\eqn{1, \ldots, K_0} have explicit \link[=pc-class]{pc} specifications (any
families, including Gaussian); lags \eqn{K_0 + 1, \ldots, K} use the
\code{tail} family.

\strong{Free mode} (known models only). An explicit list of
\code{\link[rvinecopulib:bicop_dist]{rvinecopulib::bicop_dist()}} objects with their own parameters, optionally
followed by a Gaussian ARMA tail. The rotation of a \code{bicop_dist} is
interpreted by \code{\link[rvinecopulib:bicop_dist]{rvinecopulib::bicop_dist()}} applied to the pair (earlier
variable, later variable).

The effective lag \eqn{K} is computed once when the object is created and
stored: it is the last lag at which \eqn{|\tau_k|} exceeds \code{tautol}, capped
at \code{maxlag}, and at least the number of explicit pair copulas; for a pure
AR tail it is the AR order. The fitted and simulated process is therefore
always a finite-order Markov chain.
}
